  "slug": "pangenome-graphs-bd3e",
  "summary": "Recent preprints apply graph and k-mer representations to haplotype assignment, ancestry, and relatedness without relying on variant calls.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the replacement of linear-reference coordinates and variant-call inputs with graph or k-mer representations. GRASER phases graph-aligned short reads directly against individual haplotype paths, using unique graph edges to avoid linear-reference bias. KORUS infers ancestry from k-mer Bloom filter representations without variant calls, supporting classification, admixture fractions, and population structure. The fastpmr method computes pairwise mismatch rates for relatedness inference, reported as more than 1000-fold faster than READv2 and applied to screening the global ancient DNA record. What is new is the direct use of graph paths and k-mer sets for haplotype and population-level inference. MitoArc and GapCNV address adjacent problems, namely a reproducible Nextflow workflow for human mtDNA with NUMT-aware evaluation and CNV detection in low-input sequencing data, respectively.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-23"
}
